.TH CVEC_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_destroy \- destroy a vector and clear the handle (type-inferred wrapper)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_destroy(v);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro frees the backing buffer of the vector and the container
itself, and then sets v to NULL. It leaves a handle that is already NULL
alone and frees nothing for it, so a second cvec_destroy() of the same
variable does nothing and is safe.

The macro frees everything, including the private copy of the procedure
table, through the memory management procedures that created the vector,
so it needs no allocator argument. This is the name that the
type-inferred macro layer uses; the macro is cvector_destroy(3) under
another name and behaves in the same way.

The macro clears the handle and nothing else. The companion type
variables that cvec_declare(3) and the construct macros declare stay in
scope and stay valid, so cvec_init(3) or cvec_init_mp(3) can give the
same variable a new vector later.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v  A cvec lvalue that names the vector to destroy, such as a variable or
   an element of an array, and that the macro must be able to assign to.
   The macro evaluates it exactly once, so an expression with a side
   effect, such as vs[i++], destroys and clears one element. The macro
   sets it to NULL before it returns, and does nothing for an lvalue that
   holds NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
When the vector holds pointers to heap buffers, the macro does not free
the elements one by one, so the caller must free the element data first.
This call invalidates every pointer from an earlier cvec_data_ptr(3) or
cvec_at_ptr(3).
cvec_declare_scoped(3), cvec_construct_scoped(3) and
cvec_construct_mp_scoped(3) destroy their vector at the end of the scope
by themselves, clearing the handle as they do it, so an explicit call
inside such a block is legal but not necessary.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Destroy a vector one time, through one handle. A cvec that you copy into
a second variable leaves that copy dangling, and a later destruction
through that copy is a double free.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 42);

cvec_destroy(vec);   /* vec is NULL after this call */
cvec_destroy(vec);   /* this call does nothing and is safe */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_destroy (3),
.BR cvec_construct (3),
.BR cvec_init (3),
.BR cvec_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_reset (3)
